    {h:'Five Dynasties at a Glance',items:['1. <b>Slave/Mamluk</b> (1206–1290) — Qutb-ud-din Aibak (founder); Iltutmish (silver tanka; Abbasid recognition); <b>Raziya Sultana</b> (1236–40) — first female ruler of Delhi','2. <b>Khalji</b> (1290–1320) — Alauddin Khalji (1296–1316): market reforms (4 markets); repelled Mongols; Deccan campaigns; Qutb Minar completed','3. <b>Tughlaq</b> (1320–1414) — Muhammad bin Tughlaq (capital shift to Daulatabad; token currency disaster); Firuz Shah (300 towns, canals, hospitals); Ibn Battuta visited','4. <b>Sayyid</b> (1414–51) — Weak rulers; shrunken sultanate after Timur\'s raid (1398); further decentralisation','5. <b>Lodi</b> (1451–1526) — Only Afghan dynasty; Ibrahim Lodi\'s arrogance → invited Babur → 1st Battle of Panipat (1526)']},
    {h:'Key Achievements & Cultural Impact',items:['Qutb Minar (1193 CE) — tallest brick minaret in India; Indo-Islamic architecture','Alauddin\'s 4 markets: grain, cloth, horses/cattle, general goods — controlled by Diwan-i-Riyasat','<b>Persian</b> became official language → Indo-Islamic cultural synthesis blossomed','<b>Amir Khusrau</b> (1253–1325) — pioneer of Hindavi/Urdu poetry; invented qawwali; "Parrot of India"','Firuz Shah — built canals, hospitals, rest houses; first to introduce welfare measures in sultanate']}
