    {q:'The Vesara style of temple architecture, seen at Halebidu and Belur, represents a hybrid of:',opts:['Nagara and Indo-Islamic styles','Nagara and Dravida styles','Dravida and Indo-Saracenic styles','Buddhist and Dravida styles'],ans:1,exp:'The Vesara style, prevalent in the Deccan/Karnataka region (e.g., Hoysaleswara Temple, Halebidu), is a hybrid of the Nagara (North Indian) and Dravida (South Indian) temple styles, often featuring star-shaped ground plans.',yr:'UPSC 2019'},
    {q:'A "Gopuram" is a distinguishing feature of which temple architectural style?',opts:['Nagara','Dravida','Vesara','Indo-Islamic'],ans:1,exp:'The Gopuram — a tall, ornate gateway tower at temple entrances — is a hallmark of Dravida (South Indian) temple architecture, as seen at the Meenakshi Temple, Madurai.',yr:'UPSC 2014'}
