    {h:'Key Population Statistics',items:['World population crossed <b>8 billion in November 2022</b> (UN estimate)','<b>India</b> overtook China as most populous country (~2023, per UN estimates)','<b>Asia</b> = most populous continent (home to over half of world population)','<b>Antarctica</b> = least populated (no permanent population)','Fastest urbanizing regions: <b>Asia & Africa</b>']},
    {h:'Population Distribution Factors',items:['<b>Physical factors</b>: climate, terrain, water availability, soil fertility, natural resources','<b>Economic factors</b>: industrialization, employment, infrastructure, trade routes','<b>Social/Historical</b>: political stability, historical settlement patterns, colonial legacy','★ Dense: river valleys (Nile, Ganges, Yangtze), coastal plains, temperate zones','★ Sparse: deserts, polar regions, dense rainforests, high altitude areas']},
    {h:'Urbanization Trends',items:['>55% of world population lives in urban areas (2023 estimate; projected ~68% by 2050)','Fastest urban growth: Africa & South/SE Asia','<b>Megacities</b> — cities with 10 million+ population; Tokyo = world\'s largest urban agglomeration','<b>Primate City</b> — one dominant city that is disproportionately large compared to the next city (Bangkok, Paris, Lima)','Urbanization linked to: migration (rural-urban), natural increase, administrative boundary expansion']}
